    "long_instructions": "A beach concession stand wants to predict how many people will come every day. If it’s a slow day, they could be overstocked. If it’s busy, they may run out of concessions.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n- Import the `train_test_split` function.\r\n- Split the data into training and test sets. 20% of the data should be in the test set.\r\n\r\nSave TODOs 3–5 for the next level.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nRemember that to use the `train_test_split` function, you should import it with:\r\n\r\n```\r\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `train_test_split` function takes `input` and `output` as its first two arguments. To put 20% of the data in a test set, set the `test_size` parameter to `0.2`.\r\n\r\nThe return value will be four separate lists, always in this order: `train_inputs`, `test_inputs`, `train_outputs`, `test_outputs`. Be sure to unpack all four when you call the function, or you'll get an error.\r\n\r\n```\r\ntrain_inputs, test_inputs, train_outputs, test_outputs = train_test_split(\r\n  inputs, outputs, test_size=0.2\r\n)\r\n```",
